# fanctl-io minimum duty, per fan channel.
#
# This file is read by fanctl-io itself, not by the Python above it and not by
# the web page. Nothing on the helper's command line can lower the number that
# comes out of here, and no such option will be added: the floor has to survive
# a bug in every layer above it.
#
# Format, one rule per line:
#
#	default <percent>	applies to every channel with no line of its own
#	<channel> <percent>	applies to that channel and overrides the default
#
# Channels are numbered from 1, as the chip numbers them.
#
# Two rules that matter more than the format:
#
#   * A value below the compiled-in hard floor is raised to it, and the status
#     answer says so with floor_source "file-clamped". The file can make a fan
#     spin faster than the hard floor demands. It cannot make one spin slower.
#
#   * A malformed line rejects the WHOLE file and the hard floor applies to
#     every channel, reported as floor_source "file-rejected". A typo must not
#     be a quiet way to lower the number that keeps a fan turning.
#
# EVERY LINE BELOW IS COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE.
#
# A floor is a property of a particular fan in a particular chassis: the duty
# below which that fan stalls, or spins so slowly that it stops moving air.
# Nobody can know that from here, and it is found by watching one fan on one
# machine, stepping the duty down and reading the tachometer, with somebody in
# the room. Until that has been done, the hard floor applies - which is
# deliberately higher than any fan needs, because refusing is cheap and a
# stopped fan is not.
#
# Once it has been measured, write it down here with the measurement beside it,
# the way the rest of this plugin does:
#
#	# Delta AFB0612EH on channel 1, XG 330 rev 2: still turning at 35%
#	# (1,040 rpm), stalled at 25% on 2026-09-22 with the lid off.
#	1 40
#
#default 30
